                   pa
        a simple password manager
         https://passwordass.org

  features
    - encryption implemented using age[1]
    - automatic key generation
    - automatic git tracking
    - multiple identity/recipient support
    - written in portable posix shell
    - simple to extend
    - only ~200 lines of code
    - pronounced "pah" - as in "papa"


  dependencies
    - age
    - age-keygen
    - git (optional)
    - Go 1.26+ (only to build pa-xfer)


  usage
    pa
      a simple password manager

    commands:
      [a]dd  [name] - Add a password entry.
      [d]el  [name] - Delete a password entry.
      [e]dit [name] - Edit a password entry with vi.
      [g]it  [cmd]  - Run git command in the password dir.
      [l]ist [cat]  - List all entries in a category.
      [s]how [name] - Show password for an entry.
      [m]ove [src] [name] - Rename a password entry.

    env vars:
      data directory:   export PA_DIR=~/.local/share/pa/passwords
      password length:  export PA_LENGTH=50
      password pattern: export PA_PATTERN=A-Za-z0-9_-
      disable tracking: export PA_NOGIT=


  safe transfer
    pa-xfer moves entries between pa stores while each machine keeps its own
    age identity. Transfers are a set union by name: existing destination
    names are always skipped, even when the two values differ. There is no
    overwrite mode.

    On the destination machine, obtain its public recipient file or inspect
    its fingerprint:

      $ pa-xfer recipient

    On the source machine, encrypt a binary-safe bundle directly to that
    recipient:

      $ pa-xfer export --to ./destination-recipients ./to-destination.age

    Back on the destination, verify and import it:

      $ pa-xfer verify ./to-destination.age
      $ pa-xfer import ./to-destination.age

    Use --json on any pa-xfer command for machine-readable results. Import
    requires a clean password Git repository when Git tracking is enabled.
    Successful imports create private before/after snapshots and a receipt
    under $PA_DIR/backups/<transaction-id>/.

    The pa-export and pa-import commands are compatibility entry points for
    pa-xfer. The old tab-delimited passphrase format is intentionally not
    supported because it could not preserve arbitrary bytes and wrote all
    plaintext values to a temporary file.

    See docs/transfer-protocol.md for the format and security invariants.


  installation
    $ make check
    $ make install PREFIX="$HOME/.local"


  command examples
    $ pa add test
    generate a password? [y/N]: y
    saved 'test' to the store.

    $ pa list
    test

    $ pa show test
    vJwKuEBtxBVvdR-xppTdfofIei0oLlkoSK4OCSP2bMEBsP6ahM

    $ pa edit test
    <opens $EDITOR or vi>

    $ pa mv test example

    $ pa del example
    delete password 'example'? [y/N]: y

    $ pa git log --oneline
    bbe85dc (HEAD -> main) delete 'example'
    c7f76d7 move 'test' to 'example'
    b597c04 edit 'test'
    cba20cc add 'test'
    ef76f7e initial commit


  faq
    > how does this differ from pass, passage, etc?

      pa is smaller. simpler. cleaner. plainer.
      harder. better. faster. stronger.
      more than ever, hour after hour
      work is never over

    > is pa secure?

      if you would like to understand the
      security characteristics of pa, please
      read my blog post[2], and my explanation[3].

    > why u make this?

      see [2].

    > where are my keys?

      probably the default locations:
        ~/.local/share/pa/identities
        ~/.local/share/pa/recipients

    > where are my passwords?

      probably the default location:
        ~/.local/share/pa/passwords


